  "narrative": "During the 2010s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture continued to thrive, building upon the momentum of the natural hair movement that originated in the 1960s. The decade saw a resurgence of interest in embracing afro-textured hair, with styles like the afro, cornrows, and box braids becoming increasingly popular. These traditional hairstyles not only celebrated African heritage but also provided a sense of identity and community for women who had been socialized to conform to Eurocentric beauty standards. The natural hair movement also led to the growth of protective hairstyle culture, with styles like Bantu knots and locs gaining popularity as a way to minimize breakage and promote hair health. While documentation from this era is limited, it's clear that Black and African women were using their hairstyles as a means of self-expression, empowerment, and resistance against societal beauty norms.",
